WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.120
Ethics guidance for research involving human&nbsp;
participants has been developed and disseminated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.000
by numerous organizations and agencies at&nbsp;
international, regional, and national levels over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.240
the past 50 years. Adherence to these guidelines&nbsp;
helps to promote the ethical conduct of research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.880
and enhances and protects the rights and wellbeing&nbsp;
of research participants and communities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.520
A core component of all contemporary&nbsp;
research ethics guidelines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.600
is that research should be subject to prior&nbsp;
ethical review by a competent IRBs or IRB.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.960
Such review is intended to ensure that&nbsp;
the ethical principles and practices put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.080
forward in the guidelines will be&nbsp;
followed in the proposed research.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:46.246
Standards for the IRB review system

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:46.626
All research with human participants is&nbsp;
presumptively subject to IRB oversight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.627 --> 00:00:47.064
IRBs are part of larger research participant&nbsp;
protection programs that also include training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.064 --> 00:00:47.411
for IRB members and researchers, and&nbsp;
mechanisms to ensure that IRBs work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.411 --> 00:00:47.887
efficiently and effectively. National governments&nbsp;
have the primary responsibility for ensuring that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:48.372
IRBs are subject to adequate oversight. Procedures&nbsp;
exist to ensure clear and efficient communication,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.372 --> 00:00:48.781
harmonization of standards, networking, and&nbsp;
cooperation among national committees and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:49.195
between different levels of committees, as&nbsp;
applicable. These procedures enable IRBs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.195 --> 00:00:49.680
learn about prior decisions by other IRBs that may&nbsp;
be relevant to the proposed research under review.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.280
IRBs may review different types&nbsp;
of research studies, including,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.640
but not limited to, the following:
• clinical trials&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.120
• epidemiological research
• social science research&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.800
• research on medical records&nbsp;
or other personal information&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.040
• research on stored samples
• health systems research&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.120
• implementation research

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.800
IRBs should be familiar with&nbsp;
the different methodologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.120
and ethical considerations that apply to&nbsp;
each type of proposed research they review.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.800
Composition of IRB

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.760
The IRB is constituted according to a charter&nbsp;
or other document that establishes the manner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.520
in which members and the Chair will be appointed.&nbsp;
The appointing entity ensures that the IRB has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.560
multidisciplinary and multisectoral membership,&nbsp;
that its composition is gender balanced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.320
that it reflects the social and cultural&nbsp;
diversity of the communities from which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.960
research participants are most likely to be&nbsp;
drawn, and that it includes individuals with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.120
backgrounds relevant to the areas of research&nbsp;
the committee is most likely to review.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.680
The entity establishing the IRB&nbsp;
takes the following factors into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.520
consideration when appointing members.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.880
1. Members include individuals&nbsp;
with scientific expertise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.600
including expertise in behavioral or&nbsp;
social sciences; health care providers;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:16.480
members who have expertise in legal matters&nbsp;
and/or ethics; and lay people whose primary role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.240
is to share their insights about the communities&nbsp;
from which participants are likely to be drawn.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.436
2. Lay people and other members,&nbsp;
whose primary background is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.436 --> 00:02:23.200
in health research with human&nbsp;
participants, are appointed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.720
sufficient numbers to ensure that they&nbsp;
feel comfortable voicing their views.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.640
3. In order to enhance independence, committee&nbsp;
membership includes members who are not affiliated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.360
with organizations that sponsor, fund,&nbsp;
or conduct research reviewed by the IRB.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.200
Committees are large enough to ensure&nbsp;
that multiple perspectives are brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.640
into the discussion. To this end, quorum&nbsp;
requirements provide that at least five people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.160
including at least one lay member&nbsp;
and one non-affiliated member,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.880
are present to make decisions&nbsp;
about the proposed research.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.480
IRB resources

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.600
The entity establishing the IRB supports it&nbsp;
with adequate resources, including staffing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.360
facilities, and financial resources to allow the&nbsp;
IRB to effectively carry out its responsibilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.360
As an integral part of a health research&nbsp;
institution or health system, an IRB receives:

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.280
1. support staff, adequate in number and training&nbsp;
to enable the IRB to carry out its technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.840
and administrative responsibilities;
2. adequate resources for the staff to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:31.280
fulfil its assigned functions, including office&nbsp;
space and equipment and supplies (e.g. computers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:37.200
stationery, telephones, photocopying machines,&nbsp;
shredding machine) to conduct administrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.600
business, to store committee files, and&nbsp;
to keep documents secure and confidential;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.080
3. access to appropriate space for the&nbsp;
committee to meet and adequate means for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.680
members to communicate as needed between meetings;
4. adequate financial resources to permit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.538
committee to produce high-quality work;
5. if considered necessary by the entity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.538 --> 00:03:58.640
establishing the IRB, resources necessary&nbsp;
to compensate IRB members, unless they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.800
already being compensated for their time&nbsp;
and effort on the IRB through other means.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.480
Independence of IRBs

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:12.160
Policies governing the IRB include mechanisms&nbsp;
to ensure independence of the IRB’s operations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.200
in order to protect decision making from influence&nbsp;
by any individual or entity that sponsors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.600
conducts, or hosts the research it reviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:26.000
Such policies provide at a minimum that IRB&nbsp;
members (including the Chair) remove themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.560
from the review of any research in which they or&nbsp;
close family members have a conflicting interest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:36.720
To ensure that the IRB cannot be pressured to&nbsp;
approve or disapprove particular protocols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.840
the charter, by-laws, policies and/or&nbsp;
procedural rules of the IRB provide that:

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:47.280
1. the IRB’s membership includes at least one&nbsp;
person with no connection to the organization that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.360
sponsors or conducts the research under review;
2. researchers, sponsors, and funders may attend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.320
an IRB meeting to answer questions about their&nbsp;
research protocols and associated documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.560
but they are not present when the IRB reaches&nbsp;
decisions about their proposed research;&nbsp;

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.720
3. senior decision-makers of&nbsp;
the entity creating the IRB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.320
or of any organization that sponsors or conducts&nbsp;
the research reviewed by the IRB, such as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.000
director of an institution, or his or her agent,&nbsp;
do not serve as members of the IRB or its Chair;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.720
4. the entity that establishes&nbsp;
the IRB ensures that IRB members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.920
are protected from retaliation&nbsp;
based on positions taken with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.520
respect to IRB-related matters&nbsp;
or review of research projects.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.200
Training the IRB

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:37.360
Training on the ethical aspects of&nbsp;
health-related research with human participants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.880
how ethical considerations apply&nbsp;
to different types of research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.080
and how the IRB conducts its review of research,&nbsp;
is provided to IRB members when they join the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:51.840
committee and periodically during their committee&nbsp;
service. The training provided to IRB members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.920
either directly by the appointing entity&nbsp;
or through cooperative arrangements with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.040
other IRBs or organizations that provide&nbsp;
education on research ethics, focuses on:

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:07.280
1. the role and responsibilities of the IRB,&nbsp;
and its role vis-à-vis other relevant entities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.280
according to relevant international&nbsp;
guidelines (e.g. International Council&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.840
on Harmonization Good Clinical Practice or ICH&nbsp;
GCP), national laws, and institutional policies;&nbsp;

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:23.280
2. the full range of ethical considerations&nbsp;
relevant to research with human participants;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:28.160
3. the application of such ethical&nbsp;
considerations to different types of research;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:33.840
4. basic aspects of research methodology and&nbsp;
design for members who lack such background;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.360
5. the impact of different scientific designs&nbsp;
and objectives on the ethics of a research study;&nbsp;

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.560
6. the various approaches for recognizing and&nbsp;
resolving the tensions that can arise among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.840
different ethical considerations&nbsp;
and modes of ethical reasoning.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.560
When training is supported by research&nbsp;
sponsors, mechanisms are in place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:57.760
ensure that the sponsor has no control, direct&nbsp;
or indirect, over the content of the training.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:01.360
Transparency, accountability,&nbsp;
and quality of the IRB

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.880
Mechanisms exist to make&nbsp;
IRB operations transparent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:07.280
accountable, consistent, and of high quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.600
The entity establishing the IRB employs reliable&nbsp;
means to evaluate whether the staff and members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:19.280
the IRB routinely follow the IRB’s policies,&nbsp;
rules, and written procedures, with special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.840
attention to whether the ethical considerations&nbsp;
articulated in international guidelines and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.840
national standards are being considered&nbsp;
and applied consistently and coherently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.160
Such evaluations are conducted by knowledgeable&nbsp;
and unbiased people at regular, pre-defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:40.080
intervals using a pre-defined format. The results&nbsp;
of the evaluation are of a type that can aid the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.120
IRB in reviewing its practice and appraising&nbsp;
performance, rather than apportioning blame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.760
while also assuring the public that research is&nbsp;
being reviewed according to established standards.

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:54.320
Researchers, research participants,&nbsp;
and other interested parties have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.360
a means of lodging complaints about the&nbsp;
IRB. Such complaints should be reviewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.240
by an entity other than the IRB itself, and&nbsp;
appropriate follow-up actions should be taken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.280
Researchers also have a means of&nbsp;
discussing concerns with IRB members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.160
both on general matters and in response to&nbsp;
IRB decisions on particular research studies.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.880
IRB decisions, excluding confidential information,&nbsp;
are made publicly available, through mechanisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.840
such as clinical trial registries, web&nbsp;
sites, newsletters, and bulletin boards.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.040
Ethical basis for decision-making in IRBs

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.400
The primary task of an IRB is the ethical review&nbsp;
of research protocols and their supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:40.720
documents. Approval or disapproval is based on the&nbsp;
ethical acceptability of the research, including:

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:47.440
• Scientific design and conduct of the study
Research is ethically acceptable only if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:54.320
relies on valid scientific methods. IRBs should&nbsp;
have documentation from a prior scientific review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:58.400
or should themselves determine that the&nbsp;
research methods are scientifically sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.720
and should examine the ethical implications&nbsp;
of the chosen research design or strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.040
Unless already determined by&nbsp;
a prior scientific review,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.360
IRBs should also assess how the study&nbsp;
will be conducted, the qualifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.160
of the researcher(s), the adequacy of provisions&nbsp;
made for monitoring and auditing, as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:21.840
adequacy of the study site (e.g. availability of&nbsp;
qualified staff and appropriate infrastructures).

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.920
• Risks and potential benefits
In ethically acceptable research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:32.640
risks have been minimized, both by preventing&nbsp;
potential harms and minimizing their negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.440
impacts should they occur, and are reasonable in&nbsp;
relation to the potential benefits of the study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.240
IRB members should be aware that risks&nbsp;
may occur in different dimensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.680
(e.g. physical, social,&nbsp;
financial, or psychological),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.240
and harm may occur either at an individual&nbsp;
level or at the family or population level.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.600
• Selection of study population and&nbsp;
recruitment of research participants&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.400
Ethically acceptable research ensures&nbsp;
that no group or class of persons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.840
bears more than its fair share of the&nbsp;
burdens of participation in research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:11.120
Similarly, no group should be deprived of its fair&nbsp;
share of the benefits of research. These benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:15.280
include the direct benefits of participation&nbsp;
(if any) as well as the new knowledge that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:21.360
research is designed to yield. Thus, one question&nbsp;
for research ethics review to consider is whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.920
the population that will bear the risks of&nbsp;
participating in the research is likely to benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:31.600
from the knowledge derived from the research. In&nbsp;
addition, ethically acceptable research includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:36.240
recruitment strategies that are balanced and&nbsp;
objectively describe the purpose of the research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:41.200
the risks and potential benefits of participating&nbsp;
in the research, and other relevant details.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:45.840
• Inducements, financial&nbsp;
benefits, and financial costs&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.040
It is considered ethically acceptable&nbsp;
and appropriate to reimburse individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.560
for any costs associated with participation in&nbsp;
research, including transportation, child care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:02.960
or lost wages. Many IRBs also believe that it is&nbsp;
ethically acceptable to compensate participants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:08.640
for their time. However, payments should not&nbsp;
be so large, or free medical care or other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:14.400
forms of compensation so extensive, as to induce&nbsp;
prospective participants to consent to participate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:18.720
in the research against their better judgement or&nbsp;
to compromise their understanding of the research.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:24.000
• Protection of research participants’&nbsp;
privacy and confidentiality&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.960
Invasions of privacy and breaches of&nbsp;
confidentiality are disrespectful to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:32.880
participants and can lead to feelings&nbsp;
of loss of control or embarrassment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:37.920
as well as tangible harms such as social&nbsp;
stigma, rejection by families or communities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.720
or lost opportunities such as employment&nbsp;
or housing. IRBs should therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.440
examine the precautions taken to safeguard&nbsp;
participants’ privacy and confidentiality.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:53.280
• Informed consent process
The ethical foundation of informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:59.120
consent is the principle of respect for persons.&nbsp;
Competent individuals are entitled to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:04.400
freely whether to participate in research, and to&nbsp;
make decisions based on an adequate understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:10.000
of what the research entails. Decisions for&nbsp;
children or adults who lack the mental capacity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:14.240
to provide informed consent should be made&nbsp;
by an authorized surrogate decision-maker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:19.920
IRBs should examine the process through which&nbsp;
informed consent will occur, as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.440
information that will be provided. IRBs may&nbsp;
waive the requirement of informed consent only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:31.040
when doing so is consistent with international&nbsp;
guidelines and national standards. While informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:35.760
consent to research is important, the fact that&nbsp;
a participant or surrogate may be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:40.400
consent to research does not, in itself, mean&nbsp;
that the research is ethically acceptable.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.000
• Community considerations&nbsp;

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:50.240
Research has impacts not only on the individuals&nbsp;
who participate, but also on the communities where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:56.240
the research occurs and/or to whom findings can be&nbsp;
linked. Duties to respect and protect communities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:01.760
require examining by the IRB and, as far as&nbsp;
possible, are aimed at minimizing any negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:07.200
effects on communities such as stigma or draining&nbsp;
of local capacity, and promoting, as relevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:12.320
positive effects on communities, including those&nbsp;
related to health effects or capacity development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:17.040
Researchers should actively engage with&nbsp;
communities in decision-making about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.840
design and conduct of research, including&nbsp;
the informed consent process, while being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:26.240
sensitive to and respecting the communities’&nbsp;
cultural, traditional and religious practices.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:30.532
Decision-making procedures for IRBs

00:13:30.532 --> 00:13:34.720
Decisions on research protocols&nbsp;
designated for review by the convened IRB&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:38.720
are based on a thorough and inclusive&nbsp;
process of discussion and deliberation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:43.920
Protocols involving no more than minimal&nbsp;
risk and burden to research participants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:48.960
may be reviewed on an expedited basis by one or&nbsp;
more members, rather than the full committee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:52.800
if the IRB has established written&nbsp;
procedures permitting such a procedure.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:58.720
During meetings of the IRB, members engage&nbsp;
in discussions to elicit all concerns and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:03.200
opinions related to the protocols and the&nbsp;
associated documents under consideration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:09.040
The IRB’s rules ensure that the discussions&nbsp;
are respectful of all opinions and allow for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:13.600
varied beliefs to be aired. Decisions&nbsp;
are made only by those who were present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:18.880
during the entire discussion. The Chair is&nbsp;
responsible for the decision-making process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:24.320
Researchers, funders, or others directly&nbsp;
associated with the protocol in question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.320 --> 00:14:29.120
are not present during committee&nbsp;
deliberations. IRB members recognize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:33.120
the limitations of their knowledge and&nbsp;
can seek external input when necessary.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:37.200
Decisions are arrived at through&nbsp;
either a vote or consensus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:43.200
Consensus does not require that all IRB members&nbsp;
support the decision, but that all members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:56.160
consider the decision at least acceptable and&nbsp;
no member considers the decision unacceptable.

